// Gyaan Arena fix-it lessons: for each chapter, [key idea, worked example, common trap / tip].
window.GA_LESSONS = {
  // ----- Maths -----
  "m6-hcf": ["HCF is the biggest number that divides both numbers. LCM is the smallest number both numbers divide into.", "HCF of 12 and 18: factors of 12 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12 and of 18 are 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18. The biggest common one is 6. LCM: 12 × 18 ÷ 6 = 36.", "Don't mix them up: HCF is never bigger than the smaller number, and LCM is never smaller than the bigger one."],
  "m6-int": ["Integers include negatives. Adding a negative number is the same as subtracting.", "(−7) + 4: start at −7 on the number line and move 4 steps right to get −3. And 5 − (−3) = 5 + 3 = 8.", "Two minus signs together make a plus: subtracting a negative means moving right on the number line."],
  "m6-frac": ["To add fractions with the same denominator, add only the numerators and keep the denominator.", "3/8 + 1/8 = 4/8, which simplifies to 1/2 because 4 and 8 share the factor 4. Equivalent fractions: 2/3 = 4/6 = 6/9.", "Never add the denominators. 1/4 + 1/4 is 2/4 = 1/2, not 2/8."],
  "m6-dec": ["Line up the decimal points before adding or subtracting decimals.", "4.5 + 3.8: 5 tenths + 8 tenths = 13 tenths, so write 3 and carry 1. 4 + 3 + 1 = 8, giving 8.3.", "Carry over when the tenths add up to 10 or more, just like with whole numbers."],
  "m6-ratio": ["A ratio compares two quantities. Simplify it by dividing both parts by their HCF.", "18 : 24 → the HCF is 6 → 3 : 4. Unitary method: if 4 pens cost ₹48, one pen costs ₹12, so 7 pens cost ₹84.", "Keep the order. 3 : 4 is not the same as 4 : 3."],
  "m6-mens": ["Perimeter is the distance around a shape. Area is the space inside it.", "Rectangle 8 cm by 5 cm: perimeter = 2 × (8 + 5) = 26 cm, and area = 8 × 5 = 40 cm².", "Perimeter is in cm, area is in cm². If you added, it's perimeter; if you multiplied, it's area."],
  "m7-int": ["When multiplying or dividing integers, the same signs give a positive and different signs give a negative.", "(−6) × (−4) = +24, (−6) × 4 = −24, and (−24) ÷ (−6) = +4.", "Work out the sign first, then multiply the numbers."],
  "m7-eq": ["Solve an equation by doing the same thing to both sides until x is alone.", "3x + 5 = 20 → subtract 5 from both sides: 3x = 15 → divide by 3: x = 5. Check it: 3 × 5 + 5 = 20 ✓.", "When you move a number across the equals sign, its operation flips: +5 becomes −5."],
  "m7-pct": ["Percent means 'out of 100'. x% of y = x × y ÷ 100.", "25% of 240 = 25 × 240 ÷ 100 = 60. Shortcut: 25% is a quarter, so 240 ÷ 4 = 60.", "10% is just ÷ 10, so you can build others from it: 15% = 10% + 5%."],
  "m7-exp": ["aⁿ means a multiplied by itself n times. When the bases are the same, add the powers: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ.", "3⁴ = 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 81. And 2³ × 2⁵ = 2⁸.", "3⁴ is not 3 × 4. Also, when multiplying powers you add the exponents; you don't multiply them."],
  "m7-area": ["Area of a triangle = ½ × base × height. Circle: circumference = 2πr and area = πr².", "Triangle with base 10 and height 6: ½ × 10 × 6 = 30 cm². Circle with r = 7: circumference = 2 × 22/7 × 7 = 44 cm.", "Don't forget the ½ for triangles, and check whether the question gives the radius or the diameter."],
  "m8-lin": ["Get the x terms on one side and the numbers on the other, then divide.", "5x − 3 = 2x + 9 → 5x − 2x = 9 + 3 → 3x = 12 → x = 4.", "Open brackets first: 3(x + 2) = 3x + 6, not 3x + 2."],
  "m8-sq": ["A square root asks which number times itself gives this number.", "√576: 576 ends in 6, so the root ends in 4 or 6. 24² = 576 ✓, so √576 = 24.", "Learn the squares up to 30. They make every root question quick."],
  "m8-cube": ["n³ = n × n × n. The cube root undoes it.", "∛2744: 2744 ends in 4, so the root ends in 4. 14³ = 2744 ✓.", "The last digit of a cube points to the last digit of its root (8 → 2, 7 → 3, 3 → 7, 2 → 8)."],
  "m8-cq": ["Discount is taken off the marked price. Simple interest = P × R × T ÷ 100.", "Marked price ₹800 with a 15% discount: 15% of 800 = 120, so the selling price is ₹680. SI on ₹2000 at 5% for 3 years = ₹300.", "The question may ask for the interest or the final amount. Amount = principal + interest."],
  "m8-alg": ["(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b² and (a + b)(a − b) = a² − b².", "(x + 4)² = x² + 8x + 16, and 103² = (100 + 3)² = 10000 + 600 + 9 = 10609.", "(a + b)² is not a² + b². Don't forget the middle term, 2ab."],
  "m8-exp": ["A negative power means 1 divided by the positive power: a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ.", "2⁻³ = 1/2³ = 1/8.", "A negative exponent does not make the answer negative. It flips the number into a fraction."],
  "m9-ns": ["a^(p/q) means take the qth root, then raise it to the power p. Also, √a × √b = √(ab).", "8^(2/3): the cube root of 8 is 2, and 2² = 4. √3 × √12 = √36 = 6.", "Take the root first. The numbers stay small and easy that way."],
  "m9-poly": ["Remainder theorem: dividing p(x) by (x − a) leaves a remainder of p(a).", "p(x) = x² + 2x − 3 divided by (x − 2): p(2) = 4 + 4 − 3 = 5.", "For (x + 3), use a = −3, because x + 3 = x − (−3)."],
  "m9-lin2": ["A point lies on a line if its x and y values make the equation true.", "Does (2, k) lie on 3x + y = 10? 3 × 2 + k = 10 → k = 4.", "Put x into the x place and y into the y place. Don't swap them."],
  "m9-coord": ["Quadrant signs go: I (+, +), II (−, +), III (−, −), IV (+, −). They run anticlockwise from the top right.", "(−3, 5): x is negative and y is positive, so it's in Quadrant II. (4, 0) is on the x-axis.", "If one coordinate is 0, the point is on an axis, not in a quadrant."],
  "m9-ang": ["The angles of a triangle add up to 180°. Supplementary angles add up to 180°; complementary angles add up to 90°.", "A triangle has angles 50° and 60°, so the third is 180 − 110 = 70°. The supplement of 130° is 50°.", "Remember: Complementary goes with a Corner (90°), and Supplementary goes with a Straight line (180°)."],
  "m9-stat": ["Mean = total ÷ count. Median = the middle value after sorting.", "Median of 7, 2, 9, 4, 5: sort to 2, 4, 5, 7, 9, so the middle is 5.", "Always sort before finding the median. The middle of the unsorted list is usually wrong."],
  "m10-real": ["For any two numbers, HCF × LCM = the product of the numbers.", "HCF = 6, LCM = 180, one number is 36. The other = 6 × 180 ÷ 36 = 30.", "This rule works for two numbers, not three."],
  "m10-pair": ["To solve a pair of equations, eliminate one variable by adding or subtracting them.", "x + y = 10 and x − y = 4. Adding gives 2x = 14, so x = 7 and y = 3.", "Once you find one variable, substitute it back to get the other, then check both equations."],
  "m10-quad": ["For x² − (sum)x + (product) = 0, look for two numbers with that sum and product.", "x² − 5x + 6 = 0: 2 and 3 add to 5 and multiply to 6, so x = 2 or x = 3. The sum of the roots is −b/a.", "Watch the signs. (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0 gives roots +2 and +3, not −2 and −3."],
  "m10-ap": ["The nth term of an AP is a + (n − 1)d. The sum of n terms is n/2 × [2a + (n − 1)d].", "AP 3, 7, 11, … has a = 3 and d = 4. The 10th term = 3 + 9 × 4 = 39.", "Use (n − 1)d, not nd. The first term already counts as one term."],
  "m10-trig": ["Learn the table: sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = 1/√2, sin 60° = √3/2. Cos runs the same values in reverse.", "cos 60° = sin 30° = 1/2. tan 45° = 1. sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 for every angle.", "tan = sin ÷ cos. So tan 60° = (√3/2) ÷ (1/2) = √3."],
  "m10-prob": ["Probability = favourable outcomes ÷ total outcomes.", "P(king) = 4 kings ÷ 52 cards = 1/13. P(an even number on a die) = 3/6 = 1/2.", "Count the total carefully: two coins have 4 outcomes (HH, HT, TH, TT), not 3."],
  "m10-coord": ["Distance between two points = √[(x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²].", "From (1, 2) to (4, 6): √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5.", "Square the differences before adding them. Negative differences become positive."],
  "c-pct": ["Profit % = profit ÷ cost price × 100. Selling price = CP × (100 + profit%) ÷ 100.", "CP ₹500 with a 20% profit: SP = 500 × 120 ÷ 100 = ₹600.", "A rise of x% then a fall of x% is a net loss of x²/100 %, not zero."],
  "c-tw": ["Add the work rates: if A takes a days and B takes b days, together they take ab ÷ (a + b) days.", "A takes 12 days and B takes 24: 12 × 24 ÷ 36 = 8 days.", "Add the rates (1/12 + 1/24), not the days."],
  "c-int": ["SI = PRT ÷ 100. Compound amount = P(1 + R/100)ⁿ, and CI = amount − P.", "₹1000 at 10% for 2 years: SI = ₹200, while CI = 1000 × 1.21 − 1000 = ₹210.", "CI is always at least as much as SI, because you also earn interest on the interest."],
  "c-log": ["log_b(x) asks: b to what power gives x?", "log₂ 64 = 6 because 2⁶ = 64. And log 2 + log 5 = log 10 = 1.", "log(a) + log(b) = log(ab). Logs turn multiplication into addition."],
  "c-der": ["Power rule: d/dx(axⁿ) = a·n·xⁿ⁻¹.", "d/dx(3x⁴) = 12x³. Also d/dx(sin x) = cos x and d/dx(eˣ) = eˣ.", "Multiply by the old power first, then lower the power by 1."],
  "c-series": ["Look at the differences between terms, or at the ratios if the terms grow fast.", "5, 9, 13, 17: the difference is +4, so the next term is 21. 2, 6, 18, 54 is ×3, so the next is 162.", "If the differences themselves change (+1, +2, +3…), look at the pattern in the differences."],
  "c-avg": ["Average = sum ÷ count, so sum = average × count.", "5 numbers average 20, so the sum is 100. After removing one, 4 numbers average 18, so their sum is 72. The removed number = 28.", "Convert averages to totals before adding or subtracting."],
  // ----- Science -----
  "s6-food": ["Food has carbohydrates and fats (energy), proteins (growth), and vitamins and minerals (protection).", "Rice gives carbohydrates and dal gives protein. Missing vitamin C causes scurvy; missing vitamin D causes rickets.", "Iodine turns starch blue-black. It's a simple test you can try at home."],
  "s6-sep": ["We separate mixtures by differences in size, weight or state.", "Winnowing uses wind to blow husk away. Sedimentation lets mud settle, decantation pours off the water, and filtration traps tea leaves.", "Evaporation gives us salt from sea water, because the water turns to vapour and leaves the salt behind."],
  "s6-motion": ["The SI unit of length is the metre. 1 km = 1000 m and 1 m = 100 cm.", "A car on a straight road is rectilinear motion, a fan is circular motion, and a swing is periodic motion.", "Periodic means the motion repeats after equal intervals of time."],
  "s6-light": ["Luminous objects make their own light. Opaque objects block light and make shadows.", "Glass is transparent, butter paper is translucent, and wood is opaque. A pinhole camera gives an inverted image.", "Light travels in straight lines. That's why shadows have sharp edges."],
  "s6-elec": ["Current flows only in a closed circuit. Conductors (metals) let it pass; insulators (rubber, plastic) don't.", "Cell → wire → switch → bulb → back to the cell. Close the switch and the filament glows.", "A cell has two terminals, + and −, and both must be connected."],
  "s7-nutri": ["Plants make food by photosynthesis, using sunlight, carbon dioxide, water and chlorophyll.", "CO₂ enters through the stomata, water comes up from the roots, and glucose (food) and oxygen are made.", "Plants take in CO₂ and give out O₂ during photosynthesis. Cuscuta is a parasite with no chlorophyll."],
  "s7-acid": ["Acids taste sour and turn blue litmus red. Bases feel soapy and turn red litmus blue.", "Lemon has citric acid, curd has lactic acid, and an ant sting has formic acid. Acid + base gives salt + water (neutralisation).", "Turmeric turns red with a base. An antacid is a base that neutralises extra stomach acid."],
  "s7-heat": ["Heat moves by conduction (solids), convection (liquids and gases) and radiation (no medium needed).", "A spoon in hot tea heats by conduction. Sea breezes are convection. Sunlight reaches us by radiation.", "Normal body temperature is 37 °C. Dark clothes absorb more heat."],
  "s7-resp": ["Respiration releases energy from food. Aerobic respiration uses oxygen; anaerobic doesn't.", "Yeast makes alcohol without oxygen. Tired muscles make lactic acid, which causes cramps.", "Fish use gills, earthworms breathe through their skin, and insects breathe through spiracles."],
  "s7-curr": ["Electric current has a heating effect and a magnetic effect.", "Heaters and fuses use the heating effect. A current-carrying coil around iron becomes an electromagnet.", "A fuse melts to protect the circuit when too much current flows."],
  "s8-crop": ["Kharif crops are sown in the rainy season, like paddy and maize. Rabi crops are sown in winter, like wheat and gram.", "Ploughing, sowing, adding manure, irrigation, weeding, harvesting, then storage in silos.", "Rhizobium in pulse roots fixes nitrogen, which naturally improves the soil."],
  "s8-micro": ["Microorganisms can be friends (curd, bread, medicines) or foes (diseases).", "Lactobacillus makes curd and yeast makes bread rise. Malaria comes from a protozoan, and the common cold from a virus.", "Fleming discovered penicillin and Jenner made the smallpox vaccine."],
  "s8-force": ["Pressure = force ÷ area. Force is measured in newtons and pressure in pascals.", "A sharp knife cuts better because its small area gives high pressure. Wide bag straps spread the force and lower the pressure.", "Air pressure falls as you go higher."],
  "s8-fric": ["Friction opposes motion and produces heat.", "Treads on tyres increase grip. Oil, ball bearings and wheels reduce friction.", "Friction in liquids and gases is called drag, which is why planes and fish are streamlined."],
  "s8-sound": ["Sound is made by vibrations and needs a medium. It can't travel through a vacuum.", "Frequency is in hertz (Hz) and loudness in decibels (dB). Humans hear about 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz.", "Our voice comes from vibrating vocal cords in the larynx."],
  "s9-matter": ["Matter changes state with temperature: melting, boiling, condensation, and sublimation (solid straight to gas).", "Camphor and naphthalene sublime. Water boils at 100 °C, which is 373 K. K = °C + 273.", "Evaporation causes cooling. That's why sweating cools you down."],
  "s9-cell": ["The cell is the basic unit of life. Robert Hooke first saw cells, and Robert Brown discovered the nucleus.", "Mitochondria are the powerhouse, ribosomes make proteins, and lysosomes are the 'suicide bags'.", "Plant cells have a cell wall and chloroplasts. Animal cells don't."],
  "s9-motion": ["Velocity is speed with direction. Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity.", "v = u + at. A car going from 0 to 20 m/s in 5 s accelerates at 4 m/s².", "The area under a velocity–time graph gives the displacement."],
  "s9-laws": ["Newton's laws: (1) inertia, (2) F = ma, (3) every action has an equal and opposite reaction.", "Pushing 2 kg with an acceleration of 3 m/s² needs 6 N of force. Momentum p = mv, in kg m/s.", "Mass measures inertia. That's why you lurch forward when a bus suddenly brakes."],
  "s9-grav": ["Every mass attracts every other mass. On Earth, g ≈ 9.8 m/s².", "On the Moon your weight is about 1/6 of your Earth weight, but your mass stays the same.", "Weight is a force (newtons). Mass is the amount of matter (kg)."],
  "s10-chem": ["Reaction types: combination, decomposition, displacement, double displacement, and redox.", "Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu is displacement, because the more reactive iron pushes copper out.", "Oxidation is gaining oxygen. Rusting and rancidity are both oxidation."],
  "s10-acid": ["pH below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral, and above 7 is basic.", "Baking soda is NaHCO₃, washing soda is Na₂CO₃·10H₂O, and bleaching powder is CaOCl₂.", "Plaster of Paris is CaSO₄·½H₂O. It sets hard when water is added."],
  "s10-life": ["Life processes: nutrition, respiration, transport and excretion.", "The heart has 4 chambers. Arteries carry blood away from the heart. The nephron filters blood in the kidney.", "In plants, xylem carries water and phloem carries food."],
  "s10-light": ["Concave mirrors converge light; convex mirrors diverge it and give a wider view.", "Dentists use concave mirrors and vehicles use convex rear-view mirrors. Power P = 1/f (in metres), measured in dioptres.", "A lens with f = 0.5 m has P = 1/0.5 = +2 D. Convert cm to m first!"],
  "s10-elec": ["Ohm's law: V = IR. Power P = VI. Energy is in joules or kWh.", "A 6 V battery across 3 Ω gives I = 2 A and P = 12 W. 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J.", "In series, add the resistances. In parallel, add the reciprocals."],
  "c-phy": ["Key constants: c ≈ 3 × 10⁸ m/s and g ≈ 9.8 m/s². Power is measured in watts and charge in coulombs.", "Kinetic energy = ½mv². A 2 kg ball at 3 m/s has 9 J.", "Neutrons have no charge, protons are positive and electrons are negative."],
  "c-chem": ["Avogadro's number is 6.022 × 10²³ particles in one mole.", "Carbon has atomic number 6. Fluorine is the most electronegative element. Ozone is O₃.", "Noble gases (He, Ne, Ar…) have full outer shells, so they are very unreactive."],
  "c-bio": ["DNA is a double helix (Watson and Crick). Human body cells have 46 chromosomes.", "The pancreas makes insulin. Skin is the largest organ. O negative is the universal donor.", "AB positive is the universal recipient. Don't mix it up with the universal donor."],
  "c-cs": ["Computers store everything as bits (0s and 1s). 8 bits make a byte.", "5 in binary: 4 + 1 = 101. CPU stands for Central Processing Unit. RAM is volatile, so it's wiped when the power goes off.", "HTTP is how web pages travel: HyperText Transfer Protocol."],
  // ----- English -----
  "e6-noun": ["Nouns name things. Proper nouns name a specific one (Jaipur), abstract nouns name ideas (honesty), and collective nouns name groups.", "A pack of wolves, a school of fish, a flock of sheep. Irregular plurals: child → children, mouse → mice.", "Pronouns replace nouns: Rahul → he, Rahul's → his."],
  "e6-art": ["Use 'an' before a vowel sound and 'a' before a consonant sound. 'The' points to something specific or unique.", "An honest girl (the h is silent), a university (it sounds like 'you'), the sun (there's only one).", "Go by the sound, not the letter: an hour, but a European."],
  "e6-syn": ["Synonyms are words with the same or similar meaning.", "Happy = glad, big = huge, begin = start, silent = quiet.", "Try the option in the sentence. If the meaning stays the same, it's a synonym."],
  "e6-ant": ["Antonyms are words with opposite meanings.", "Hot ↔ cold, ancient ↔ modern, arrive ↔ depart, victory ↔ defeat.", "Don't pick a synonym by mistake. The antonym should flip the meaning."],
  "e7-tense": ["Tense shows when an action happens: past, present or future, and whether it's simple, continuous or perfect.", "She goes to school every day (habit). Look, it is raining (right now). I have finished (just completed).", "Look for clue words: yesterday → past, every day → simple present, already → present perfect."],
  "e7-prep": ["Prepositions show place and time: in, on, at, since, for.", "In 2012 (a year), on Monday (a day), at 5 o'clock (an exact time). Since 2015 (a starting point), for two hours (a length of time).", "'Since' needs a starting point and 'for' needs a duration."],
  "e7-adj": ["Adjectives describe nouns. Adverbs describe verbs, often ending in -ly.", "The tall boy (adjective) runs quickly (adverb). good → better → best, bad → worse → worst.", "Use 'than' with comparatives (taller than) and 'the' with superlatives (the tallest)."],
  "e7-spell": ["Many tricky spellings follow patterns. 'i before e except after c' works for receive and believe.", "neCeSSary has one C and two S's. sepArate has 'a rat' in it. February has an r after the b.", "Say the word slowly in syllables, such as Feb-ru-ar-y and lib-rar-y."],
  "e8-voice": ["Passive voice: object + form of 'be' + past participle (+ by the subject).", "Ravi wrote a letter → A letter was written by Ravi. They are building a house → A house is being built by them.", "Keep the tense the same: wrote (past) → was written, sings (present) → is sung."],
  "e8-idiom": ["Idioms mean something different from their literal words.", "'A piece of cake' means very easy. 'Spill the beans' means reveal a secret. 'Under the weather' means feeling ill.", "Learn idioms as whole phrases, and picture the meaning rather than the words."],
  "e8-sva": ["A singular subject takes a singular verb, and a plural subject takes a plural verb.", "Each of the students has a book. The news is good. Neither Ram nor his friends are coming (the verb follows the nearer subject).", "Words between the subject and the verb don't change it: One of my friends lives in Chennai."],
  "e8-homo": ["Homophones sound the same but have different spellings and meanings.", "Hear (with your ears) vs here (this place). They're = they are, their = belongs to them, there = that place.", "Expand contractions to check: does 'they are' fit? Then write they're."],
  "e9-rep": ["In reported speech, shift the tense back and change the pronouns.", "He said, \"I am tired.\" → He said that he was tired. 'Tomorrow' becomes 'the next day'.", "Universal truths don't shift: The teacher said that the earth goes round the sun."],
  "e9-modal": ["Modals show ability (can), permission (may), duty (must), advice (should) and possibility (may or might).", "May I come in? You must obey rules. You should see a doctor. It may rain.", "'Ought' always takes 'to': We ought to respect our elders."],
  "e9-det": ["Use 'much' and 'little' with uncountable nouns (milk, money), and 'many' and 'few' with countable ones (apples, friends).", "A few friends means some, which is positive. Few friends means almost none, which is negative.", "Use 'some' in offers and requests: Would you like some tea?"],
  "e9-bee": ["Beehive (Class 9) mixes stories, biographies and poems.", "'The Fun They Had' by Isaac Asimov features a mechanical teacher. Evelyn Glennie is a deaf percussionist. Bismillah Khan played the shehnai.", "Match each poem to its poet: 'The Road Not Taken' is by Robert Frost, and 'Wind' is by Subramania Bharati."],
  "e10-ff": ["First Flight (Class 10) teaches values through stories and poems.", "Lencho asked God for 100 pesos but got 70. Anne Frank called her diary Kitty. Wanda claimed to have a hundred dresses.", "Robert Frost wrote 'Dust of Snow' and 'Fire and Ice'. 'Long Walk to Freedom' is about Nelson Mandela."],
  "e10-conn": ["Connectors join ideas: because (reason), although (contrast), unless (condition), so (result).", "Although he worked hard, he failed. You'll miss the bus unless you hurry.", "'Who' is for people, 'which' is for things, and 'where' is for places."],
  "e10-vocab": ["Work out a word's meaning from the sentence around it.", "'Reluctant' means unwilling. 'Abundant' means plentiful. 'Fragile' means easily broken.", "Put your option back into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you've got it."],
  "c-vocab": ["Advanced words often have Latin roots, and knowing the root helps.", "Ubiquitous means found everywhere. Ephemeral means short-lived. Frugal means careful with money.", "Make a sentence with each new word. It sticks much better than a list."],
  "c-ows": ["One-word substitution swaps a whole description for a single word.", "One who knows everything = omniscient. Fear of heights = acrophobia. A place where bees are kept = apiary.", "Learn the parts: omni = all, phobia = fear, bio = life, graphy = writing."],
  "c-err": ["Error spotting tests grammar rules: agreement, tense, prepositions and idioms.", "He doesn't like (not don't). Look forward to meeting (not meet). Furniture is uncountable, so never 'furnitures'.", "'Scarcely' and 'hardly' pair with 'when', not 'than'."],
  "c-phrasal": ["Phrasal verbs are a verb plus a small word, with a new meaning.", "Put off = postpone. Call off = cancel. Turn down = reject. Run out of = have none left.", "The small word changes everything: look after (take care of) vs look up (search)."],
  // ----- General knowledge -----
  "g6-india": ["Know our national symbols: the tiger, the peacock and the lotus.", "The national song is Vande Mataram and the anthem is Jana Gana Mana. India has 28 states, and the Ashoka Chakra has 24 spokes.", "Don't mix up the national song and the national anthem."],
  "g6-world": ["There are 7 continents. Asia is the largest and the Pacific is the largest ocean.", "Everest is the tallest mountain, the Sahara is the largest hot desert, and the Nile flows through Africa.", "Antarctica is the coldest continent, but the Sahara is the largest hot desert."],
  "g7-sport": ["A cricket team has 11 players, and the Olympic flag has 5 rings.", "Abhinav Bindra won India's first individual Olympic gold (shooting, 2008). Neeraj Chopra won gold in javelin.", "Dhyan Chand is the hockey legend, and Viswanathan Anand was world chess champion five times."],
  "g7-people": ["Mahatma Gandhi is the Father of the Nation, and Jawaharlal Nehru was the first Prime Minister.", "A.P.J. Abdul Kalam is the Missile Man. Rabindranath Tagore wrote Gitanjali. Rakesh Sharma was the first Indian in space.", "Indira Gandhi was India's first woman Prime Minister."],
  "g8-history": ["Key years: 1757 Plassey, 1857 First War of Independence, 1919 Jallianwala Bagh, 1930 Dandi March, 1942 Quit India, 1947 Independence.", "Plassey (1757) began British rule in Bengal. The Dandi March protested the salt tax.", "Put these dates on a timeline. The order helps you remember them."],
  "g8-space": ["ISRO is in Bengaluru. Chandrayaan-3 landed near the Moon's south pole in 2023.", "Sputnik 1 was the first satellite and Neil Armstrong was the first on the Moon. Jupiter is the largest planet.", "Mars is the Red Planet. Venus is the hottest planet, even though Mercury is closer to the Sun."],
  "g9-const": ["The Constitution came into effect on 26 January 1950, which we celebrate as Republic Day. B.R. Ambedkar led the drafting.", "Fundamental Rights are in Part III, and there are 11 Fundamental Duties. The voting age is 18.", "The President is head of state. The Prime Minister heads the government."],
  "g9-geo": ["The Ganga is India's longest river and Rajasthan is the largest state by area.", "Indira Point is the southernmost point, the Sundarbans are in West Bengal, and the Thar Desert is in Rajasthan.", "Kanyakumari is the southern tip of the mainland, but Indira Point (Nicobar) is further south."],
  "g10-org": ["The UN was founded in 1945 and is based in New York. WHO is based in Geneva.", "The IMF is in Washington, D.C. India hosted the G20 summit in 2023. Japan's currency is the yen.", "Geneva hosts many UN agencies, but the UN headquarters itself is in New York."],
  "g10-daily": ["Science is everywhere at home: fizzy drinks contain CO₂ and cooking gas is LPG (butane and propane).", "Sunlight helps your skin make vitamin D. Diamond is the hardest natural substance. Pure water has pH 7.", "AB+ can receive from everyone, while O− can give to everyone."],
  "c-econ": ["The RBI was set up in 1935 and sets the repo rate. GST started on 1 July 2017.", "NITI Aayog replaced the Planning Commission in 2015. SEBI regulates the stock markets.", "Every note except ₹1 is signed by the RBI Governor. The ₹1 note is signed by the Finance Secretary."],
  "c-tech": ["Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web, and Linus Torvalds created Linux.", "NPCI built UPI. N.R. Narayana Murthy co-founded Infosys. Bitcoin's creator used the name Satoshi Nakamoto.", "The internet is the network; the web is the pages that run on it."],
};

// Lessons for the new NCERT books (Ganita Prakash, Curiosity, Poorvi, Ganita Manjari, Exploration, Kaveri).
(function (L) {
  const same = { "m7-dec": "m6-dec", "m8-prop": "m6-ratio", "s7-light": "s6-light", "s9-sound": "s8-sound", "s8-curr": "s7-curr", "s7-curr": "s6-elec", "m7-common": "m6-hcf", "m8-area": "m7-area", "m10-stat": "m9-stat", "m9-prob": "m10-prob", "m9-seq": "m10-ap", "m7-tri": "m9-ang", "m7-dots": "m9-stat", "m8-dots": "m9-stat", "m9-ident": "m8-alg" };
  const old = { ...L };
  for (const k in same) L[k] = old[same[k]];
  Object.assign(L, {
    "m6-pat": ["Mathematics is the study of patterns. Odd numbers, square numbers and triangular numbers each grow by a rule you can spot.", "Add the first few odd numbers: 1, then 1 plus 3 is 4, then plus 5 is 9, then plus 7 is 16. These are the square numbers, so the first n odd numbers add up to n squared.", "Find the rule before you guess. Look at the gaps between terms: equal gaps, growing gaps, or doubling each time."],
    "m6-ang": ["An angle is the amount of turn between two arms. Acute is less than 90 degrees, right is exactly 90, obtuse is between 90 and 180, straight is 180, and reflex is more than 180.", "At 3 o'clock the clock hands are 3 hour-marks apart. Each mark is 30 degrees, so the angle is 3 times 30, which is 90 degrees, a right angle.", "Read a protractor from the zero that sits on one arm of the angle, otherwise you may read 120 instead of 60."],
    "m6-play": ["Number Play is about noticing surprising patterns in numbers, like digit sums, palindromes and the Collatz rule.", "Collatz rule: if a number is even halve it, if odd multiply by 3 and add 1. Starting at 6 we get 3, then 10, 5, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1.", "A palindrome reads the same backwards, like 343. Check the first and last digits first."],
    "m6-data": ["Data can be shown with tally marks, pictographs and bar graphs. Always read the key or scale first.", "If one apple picture stands for 5 apples, then 7 pictures show 7 times 5, which is 35 apples. Half a picture would be 2 and a half more.", "The most common mistake is counting pictures and forgetting to multiply by the key."],
    "m6-cons": ["A compass draws circles: every point on the circle is the same distance, the radius, from the centre.", "To draw a circle of radius 4 centimetres, open the compass to 4 centimetres on a ruler, fix the point and turn. Its diameter will be 8 centimetres.", "The diameter is twice the radius, not equal to it."],
    "m6-sym": ["A line of symmetry folds a shape into two matching halves. Rotational symmetry means the shape looks the same after part of a turn.", "A square has 4 lines of symmetry: two through opposite corners and two through the middles of opposite sides. It also looks the same every 90 degrees.", "A rectangle has only 2 lines of symmetry. Its diagonals are not lines of symmetry."],
    "m7-large": ["In the Indian system we group digits as ones, thousands, lakhs and crores. 1 lakh is 1,00,000 and 1 crore is 100 lakh.", "4 crore 25 lakh is written 4,25,00,000. The digit 4 is in the crore place.", "Remember the conversions: 10 lakh is 1 million, and 100 crore is 1 billion."],
    "m7-arith": ["In an expression, brackets come first, then multiplication and division, then addition and subtraction.", "12 plus 3 times 4: multiply first, 3 times 4 is 12, then add 12 to get 24. But with brackets, (12 plus 3) times 4 is 60.", "Don't just work left to right. Multiplication is done before addition unless brackets say otherwise."],
    "m7-exp": ["A letter-number like x stands for any number. An expression such as 3x plus 5 gives a value once we know x.", "If x is 4, then 3x plus 5 is 3 times 4 plus 5, which is 17. Like terms add up: 4a plus 3a minus 2a is 5a.", "3x means 3 times x, not the digits 3 and x side by side. When x is 4, 3x is 12, not 34."],
    "m7-par": ["When a line crosses two parallel lines, corresponding angles and alternate angles are equal, and co-interior angles add to 180 degrees.", "If one angle at the crossing is 70 degrees, the corresponding angle at the other line is also 70, and the co-interior angle is 110.", "Vertically opposite angles are equal, while angles on a straight line add to 180."],
    "m7-play": ["Odd plus odd is even, even plus even is even, and odd plus even is odd. Count the odd numbers to know if a sum is odd.", "Add 3 odd numbers and 2 even numbers. Three odds make an odd total, so the whole sum is odd.", "For divisibility by 9, add the digits. If the digit sum divides by 9, so does the number."],
    "m7-frac": ["To multiply fractions, multiply the tops and multiply the bottoms. To divide, multiply by the reciprocal.", "3 quarters of 36: 36 divided by 4 is 9, times 3 is 27. And 2 thirds divided by 4 fifths is 2 thirds times 5 quarters, which is 10 twelfths, or 5 sixths.", "Flip only the second fraction when dividing, never the first."],
    "m7-twin": ["Congruent triangles have exactly the same shape and size. We can prove it with SSS, SAS, ASA or RHS.", "If two sides and the angle between them match in two triangles, they are congruent by SAS.", "Three equal angles, AAA, is not enough. The triangles could be different sizes."],
    "m7-int": ["When multiplying integers, same signs give a positive answer and different signs give a negative answer.", "Minus 6 times minus 3 is plus 18, because the signs are the same. Minus 24 divided by 4 is minus 6.", "Brahmagupta's rule: a debt times a debt is a fortune. Two negatives multiply to a positive."],
    "m7-dec2": ["When multiplying decimals, multiply as whole numbers, then count the decimal places in both numbers.", "0.3 times 0.2: 3 times 2 is 6, and there are 2 decimal places in total, so the answer is 0.06.", "Check the size: multiplying by a number less than 1 makes the answer smaller."],
    "m7-tile": ["A tiling covers a flat surface with no gaps or overlaps. The angles meeting at each point must add up to 360 degrees.", "Each angle of a regular hexagon is 120 degrees. Three hexagons meet at a point because 3 times 120 is 360.", "Regular pentagons can't tile alone: 108 degrees doesn't divide 360 exactly."],
    "m7-eq": ["To find the unknown, do the same operation to both sides of the equation until the letter is alone.", "4x plus 7 equals 31. Subtract 7 from both sides to get 4x equals 24, then divide by 4: x is 6.", "Undo the steps in reverse order: first remove the added number, then divide."],
    "m8-sq": ["A square number is n times n. A cube number is n times n times n. Roots undo them.", "The square root of 625 is 25 because 25 times 25 is 625. The cube root of 343 is 7 because 7 times 7 times 7 is 343.", "A perfect square never ends in 2, 3, 7 or 8, which is a quick way to rule out answers."],
    "m8-exp": ["Powers are repeated multiplication. Same base: multiply means add the powers, divide means subtract them.", "2 to the 5 times 2 to the 3 is 2 to the 8. And 5 to the 6 divided by 5 to the 4 is 5 squared, which is 25.", "A negative power means one over: 2 to the minus 3 is 1 over 8, not minus 8."],
    "m8-story": ["Different civilisations wrote numbers in different ways. Our Hindu–Arabic system uses place value with base 10 and a zero.", "In base 2, 1011 means 8 plus 0 plus 2 plus 1, which is 11. In Roman numerals, XIV means 10 plus 4, which is 14.", "In Roman numerals, a smaller symbol before a bigger one is subtracted: IV is 4, IX is 9."],
    "m8-quad": ["The angles of any quadrilateral add up to 360 degrees. In a parallelogram, opposite angles are equal and neighbouring angles add to 180.", "Three angles are 80, 95 and 105 degrees. They add to 280, so the fourth angle is 360 minus 280, which is 80 degrees.", "Use 360 for quadrilaterals, not 180, which is for triangles."],
    "m8-play": ["Number Play shows quick tricks: the sum of consecutive numbers, and divisibility tests using digit sums.", "5 consecutive numbers from 12: 12, 13, 14, 15, 16. Their sum is 5 times the middle number 14, which is 70.", "For an odd count of consecutive numbers, the sum is count times the middle number."],
    "m8-alg": ["The distributive property: a times (b plus c) equals a times b plus a times c. Identities like (a plus b) squared come from it.", "25 times 104 is 25 times 100 plus 25 times 4, which is 2500 plus 100, giving 2600.", "(x plus 3) squared is x squared plus 6x plus 9. Don't forget the middle term."],
    "m8-cq": ["A percentage is a fraction out of 100. To find a percent of a number, multiply by the percent and divide by 100.", "3 quarters is 75 percent. 20 percent of 450 is 450 times 20 divided by 100, which is 90.", "A discount is taken from the marked price; the selling price is what's left after the discount."],
    "m8-pyth": ["In a right triangle, the square on the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares on the other two sides. Baudhayana described this long ago.", "Legs 6 and 8: 36 plus 64 is 100, and the square root of 100 is 10, so the hypotenuse is 10.", "The hypotenuse is always the longest side, opposite the right angle. Adding the legs does not give it."],
    "m8-prop2": ["To share in a ratio, add the parts, find one part, then multiply. In inverse proportion, one quantity goes up as the other goes down.", "Share 600 rupees in the ratio 2 to 3. There are 5 parts, so one part is 120. The shares are 240 and 360.", "More workers finish faster: 4 workers in 6 days means 8 workers take 3 days, not 12."],
    "m8-geo": ["Some shapes repeat themselves at smaller and smaller scales; these are fractals. Solids have faces, edges and vertices linked by Euler's formula.", "A cube has 6 faces, 8 vertices and 12 edges. 6 plus 8 minus 12 is 2, matching F plus V minus E equals 2.", "Count edges carefully: each edge is shared by two faces."],
    "m8-play2": ["Algebra explains number tricks. Write the starting number as x and follow each step.", "Think of x, double it to 2x, add 10, halve it to x plus 5, take away x: the answer is always 5.", "On a calendar, the 9 dates in a 3 by 3 block add to 9 times the middle date."],
    "m9-coord": ["Every point on a plane has coordinates (x, y). The signs tell us the quadrant, and the distance formula comes from Pythagoras.", "From (1, 2) to (4, 6): the horizontal gap is 3 and the vertical gap is 4, so the distance is 5.", "Points with y equal to 0 sit on the x-axis, not in any quadrant."],
    "m9-poly": ["A linear polynomial looks like ax plus b. Its graph is a straight line and it grows by the same amount each step.", "For p of x equals 3x minus 6, p of 4 is 12 minus 6, which is 6. Its zero is where 3x minus 6 is 0, so x is 2.", "The zero of ax plus b is minus b over a. Watch the sign."],
    "m9-circ": ["A chord joins two points on a circle; the diameter is the longest chord. The perpendicular from the centre bisects a chord.", "An arc that makes 100 degrees at the centre makes half of that, 50 degrees, at any point on the rest of the circle.", "The angle in a semicircle is always 90 degrees."],
    "m9-meas": ["Perimeter is the distance around; area is the space inside. Heron's formula finds a triangle's area from its three sides.", "For sides 13, 14 and 15, s is 21. The area is the square root of 21 times 8 times 7 times 6, which is 84.", "π is irrational. 22 over 7 is only an approximation."],
    "m9-prop": ["A proposition is a statement that is true or false. The converse of 'if p then q' is 'if q then p', and it may not be true.", "If a number is divisible by 4, it is even. The converse, if a number is even it is divisible by 4, is false: 6 is a counterexample.", "One counterexample is enough to show a statement is false."],
    "m9-stat": ["The mean is the total divided by the count. The median is the middle value after sorting.", "For 4, 8, 6, 2, 10 the total is 30, so the mean is 6. Sorted, the middle value is also 6.", "Always sort the data before finding the median."],
    "m9-algo": ["An algorithm is a clear list of steps that always finishes with an answer. Euclid's algorithm finds the HCF by repeated division.", "HCF of 48 and 18: 48 divided by 18 leaves 12, 18 divided by 12 leaves 6, 12 divided by 6 leaves 0. The HCF is 6.", "Keep dividing the previous divisor by the remainder until the remainder is zero."],
    "m9-quad": ["Special quadrilaterals have special diagonals. In a parallelogram they bisect each other; in a rectangle they are equal; in a rhombus they meet at right angles.", "The line joining the mid-points of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and half of it.", "Every square is a rectangle and a rhombus, but not every rhombus is a square."],
    "m9-lin2": ["An equation like ax plus by equals c has many solutions, and they all lie on one straight line.", "For 2x plus 3y equals 12, when x is 3, 3y is 6, so y is 2. The point (3, 2) is on the line.", "Substitute the given value first, then solve for the other variable."],
    "m9-vol": ["Volume measures space inside a solid; surface area covers its outside.", "A cuboid 5 by 4 by 3 has volume 60 cubic centimetres and surface area 2 times (20 plus 12 plus 15), which is 94 square centimetres.", "Volume uses cubic units and area uses square units. Don't mix them."],
    "m10-poly": ["The zeroes of a polynomial are where its value becomes zero. For a quadratic, the sum of zeroes is minus b over a and the product is c over a.", "For x squared minus 5x plus 6, the zeroes are 2 and 3. Their sum 5 equals minus b over a, and the product 6 equals c over a.", "Watch the sign: the sum is minus b over a, not b over a."],
    "m10-tri": ["Similar triangles have equal angles and proportional sides. If a line is parallel to one side, it divides the other two in the same ratio.", "In triangle ABC, if DE is parallel to BC, then AD over DB equals AE over EC.", "Similar is not the same as congruent. Similar triangles can be different sizes."],
    "m10-htrig": ["Heights and distances use tan: opposite side over adjacent side, from the angle of elevation.", "From 20 metres away, the top of a tower is at 45 degrees. tan 45 is 1, so the height is 20 metres.", "At 60 degrees, height equals distance times root 3. At 30 degrees, it's distance divided by root 3."],
    "m10-circ": ["A tangent touches a circle at exactly one point and is perpendicular to the radius there.", "From a point outside a circle, two tangents can be drawn, and they are equal in length.", "A secant cuts the circle at two points; a tangent touches at one."],
    "m10-arc": ["A sector is a slice of a circle. Its area is the angle over 360, times π r squared.", "A 90 degree sector of radius 14 has area one quarter of 22 over 7 times 196, which is 154 square centimetres.", "Arc length uses 2 π r, but sector area uses π r squared."],
    "m10-vol": ["Combined solids: add the surface areas you can see and add the volumes of the parts.", "A cylinder of radius 7 and height 10 has volume 22 over 7 times 49 times 10, which is 1540 cubic centimetres.", "Leave out the faces that are joined together when finding the outer surface area."],
    "s6-wonder": ["Science starts with curiosity. We ask a question, make a guess called a hypothesis, test it fairly, and look at the results.", "Does a plant need sunlight? Keep two identical pots, put one in the dark, and change nothing else. Compare them after a week.", "In a fair test, change only one thing at a time."],
    "s6-div": ["Living things vary a lot. Plants can be herbs, shrubs or trees, and their roots and leaves follow patterns.", "Plants with a taproot, like mustard, usually have net-like veins in their leaves. Grasses have fibrous roots and parallel veins.", "Match the roots to the leaves: taproot goes with reticulate venation."],
    "s6-food": ["A balanced diet has carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals, fibre and water.", "Rice gives carbohydrates, dal gives protein, and lemon gives vitamin C, which prevents scurvy.", "Eat mindfully: slowly, in the right amounts, and not too much junk food."],
    "s6-mag": ["Magnets attract iron, nickel and cobalt. Like poles repel and unlike poles attract.", "A freely hanging bar magnet always rests pointing north and south. That's how a compass works.", "Repulsion is the sure test of a magnet, because a magnet attracts plain iron too."],
    "s6-mat": ["Materials are grouped by properties: hard or soft, shiny or dull, soluble or insoluble, transparent or opaque.", "Sugar dissolves in water, so it's soluble. Sand does not, so it's insoluble.", "Metals are lustrous and conduct heat, which is why cooking pans are made of metal."],
    "s6-temp": ["Temperature tells how hot or cold something is. We measure it in degrees Celsius with a thermometer.", "Normal body temperature is about 37 degrees Celsius. A clinical thermometer reads from 35 to 42 degrees.", "Don't use a clinical thermometer for boiling water; use a laboratory thermometer."],
    "s6-water": ["Water exists as ice, liquid water and vapour. Heating and cooling change its state.", "Wet clothes dry because water evaporates. Drops form on a cold glass because water vapour from the air condenses.", "Evaporation happens at any temperature, from the surface. Boiling happens at 100 degrees."],
    "s6-sep": ["We separate mixtures using differences in size, weight or how things dissolve.", "Salt is got from sea water by evaporation. Husk is separated from grain by winnowing.", "Use filtration for insoluble solids in a liquid; evaporation for dissolved solids."],
    "s6-living": ["Living things grow, breathe, need food, respond to stimuli, reproduce and die.", "A seed germinates into a seedling when it gets water, air and warmth.", "Some non-living things move, like a car. Movement alone doesn't prove something is alive."],
    "s6-treas": ["Nature's treasures include air, water, soil, forests, minerals and fossil fuels.", "Sunlight and wind are renewable. Coal and petroleum took millions of years to form and will run out.", "Using resources wisely means reducing waste, reusing and recycling."],
    "s6-earth": ["The Sun is a star. Planets, moons and stars make up the night sky beyond Earth.", "Saptarishi, the Big Dipper, points towards the Pole Star, which shows the north.", "The Moon doesn't make its own light; it reflects sunlight."],
    "s7-sci": ["Scientific ideas change when new evidence appears. That's what makes science ever-evolving.", "People once believed the Sun went round the Earth. Careful observations showed the Earth goes round the Sun.", "A good experiment changes one variable and keeps others the same."],
    "s7-acid": ["Acids taste sour and turn blue litmus red. Bases feel soapy and turn red litmus blue.", "Turmeric turns red with a base like soap. An acid and a base neutralise each other to make salt and water.", "Never taste chemicals in the lab. Use indicators instead."],
    "s7-metal": ["Metals are shiny, malleable, ductile, sonorous and good conductors. Non-metals are usually dull and brittle.", "Mercury is a liquid metal, and bromine is a liquid non-metal.", "Metal oxides are basic; non-metal oxides are acidic."],
    "s7-change": ["In a physical change no new substance forms. In a chemical change a new substance forms.", "Melting ice is physical. Burning paper or rusting iron is chemical.", "Galvanisation coats iron with zinc to stop rust."],
    "s7-adol": ["Adolescence is the time from about 10 to 19 years when the body grows and changes. Hormones from glands control these changes.", "During puberty the voice box grows, height increases quickly, and emotions can change often.", "Eat well, sleep enough, stay clean and talk to trusted adults when worried."],
    "s7-heat": ["Heat travels by conduction in solids, convection in liquids and gases, and radiation through space.", "Sea breezes blow during the day because land heats faster than water.", "Dark colours absorb more heat; light colours reflect it."],
    "s7-time": ["Speed is distance divided by time. Time is measured in seconds.", "A car travels 120 kilometres in 2 hours, so its speed is 60 kilometres per hour.", "A pendulum's time period depends on its length, not the bob's weight."],
    "s7-resp": ["Animals get energy by digesting food and breathing. Digestion starts in the mouth and finishes in the small intestine.", "Villi in the small intestine absorb digested food. Fish breathe with gills, and earthworms through their skin.", "Most digestion happens in the small intestine, not the stomach."],
    "s7-nutri": ["Plants make food by photosynthesis using sunlight, water, carbon dioxide and chlorophyll.", "Water rises through the xylem and food moves through the phloem.", "Plants also respire day and night, taking in oxygen."],
    "s7-curr": ["A circuit needs a cell, wires and a closed path for current to flow.", "In a cell symbol, the longer line is the positive terminal. Closing the switch lights the bulb.", "Insulators like rubber don't let current flow."],
    "s7-esm": ["The Moon reflects sunlight. Its phases repeat about every 29 and a half days.", "A solar eclipse happens when the Moon blocks the Sun. A lunar eclipse happens when Earth's shadow falls on the Moon.", "Never look at a solar eclipse with bare eyes."],
    "s8-inv": ["Investigations test a hypothesis by changing one variable and measuring another.", "To see if temperature affects dissolving, change only the water temperature and measure how much sugar dissolves.", "The independent variable is what you change; the dependent variable is what you measure."],
    "s8-micro": ["Microorganisms are too small to see without a microscope. Some are helpful and some cause disease.", "Lactobacillus turns milk into curd, and yeast makes bread rise. Viruses cause the common cold.", "Antibiotics work on bacteria, not on viruses."],
    "s8-health": ["Health means physical, mental and social well-being. Communicable diseases spread; non-communicable ones don't.", "Malaria is spread by the female Anopheles mosquito. Diabetes is non-communicable.", "Vaccines prevent diseases by building immunity before infection."],
    "s8-force": ["A force is a push or a pull. It can change speed, direction or shape. Friction opposes motion.", "Magnetic force and gravity act without touching; friction and muscular force need contact.", "Friction always acts opposite to the direction of motion."],
    "s8-press": ["Pressure is force divided by area. Air flows from high pressure to low pressure, creating winds.", "Warm air rises and leaves a low-pressure region. Strong low pressure over warm seas can grow into a cyclone.", "Wider area means less pressure, which is why bag straps are wide."],
    "s8-part": ["All matter is made of tiny particles that are always moving. Spaces between them decide whether it's solid, liquid or gas.", "The smell of incense spreads across a room by diffusion, which is fastest in gases.", "Heating makes particles move faster; it doesn't make them bigger."],
    "s8-elem": ["An element has only one kind of atom. A compound has elements joined chemically. A mixture has substances simply mixed.", "Oxygen is an element, water is a compound, and air is a mixture.", "Mixtures can be separated by physical methods; compounds cannot."],
    "s8-sol": ["A solution is a solute dissolved in a solvent. Water dissolves so much that it's called the universal solvent.", "In salt water, salt is the solute and water is the solvent. When no more salt dissolves, it's saturated.", "Heating usually lets more solid dissolve."],
    "s8-light": ["Mirrors reflect light and lenses refract it. Concave mirrors and convex lenses can focus light.", "Rear-view mirrors are convex because they show a wider view. A magnifying glass is a convex lens.", "Concave lenses spread light out; convex lenses bring it together."],
    "s8-sky": ["We measure time using the sky: a day is one rotation of Earth, a year is one trip around the Sun.", "Lunar calendars follow the Moon's phases of about 29 and a half days. A leap year adds a day every four years.", "Time zones exist because different places face the Sun at different times."],
    "s8-harm": ["In nature, producers, consumers and decomposers depend on each other in food chains and food webs.", "Grass, deer, tiger: grass is the producer, the deer is a herbivore and the tiger is a carnivore.", "Energy flows from the Sun to plants to animals."],
    "s8-home": ["Earth has air, liquid water and the right temperature for life.", "The ozone layer blocks harmful ultraviolet rays, and the greenhouse effect keeps Earth warm.", "Too much carbon dioxide from burning fuels makes Earth warmer."],
    "s9-exp": ["Measurement needs a number and a unit. SI units include the metre, kilogram and second.", "A metre scale has a least count of 1 millimetre. A vernier caliper measures smaller lengths more precisely.", "Repeat readings and take the average to reduce errors."],
    "s9-cell": ["The cell is the basic unit of life. Mitochondria release energy, and the nucleus controls the cell.", "Plant cells have a cell wall and chloroplasts; animal cells do not.", "Robert Hooke saw cells first; Robert Brown discovered the nucleus."],
    "s9-tissue": ["A tissue is a group of similar cells doing one job. Plants have meristematic and permanent tissues; animals have epithelial, connective, muscular and nervous tissues.", "Blood is a connective tissue. Neurons make up nervous tissue.", "Tendons join muscle to bone; ligaments join bone to bone."],
    "s9-motion": ["Motion is described by distance, displacement, speed, velocity and acceleration.", "A car going from 10 to 30 metres per second in 5 seconds has an acceleration of 4 metres per second squared.", "Velocity has a direction; speed doesn't."],
    "s9-mix": ["Mixtures are separated using their properties: magnetism, solubility, boiling points or density.", "Inks are separated by chromatography; cream from milk by centrifugation; alcohol from water by fractional distillation.", "Choose the method based on the property that's different."],
    "s9-laws": ["Newton's laws: objects keep their state of motion, force equals mass times acceleration, and every action has an equal and opposite reaction.", "A 2 kilogram ball accelerating at 3 metres per second squared needs a 6 newton force.", "Inertia depends on mass, not speed."],
    "s9-work": ["Work equals force times displacement. Energy is the ability to do work, measured in joules.", "Lifting a 10 newton bag by 2 metres does 20 joules of work. Kinetic energy is half m v squared.", "No displacement means no work, even if you push hard."],
    "s9-atom": ["Atoms have protons and neutrons in a tiny nucleus with electrons around it.", "Carbon has atomic number 6, so 6 protons. With 6 neutrons, its mass number is 12.", "Atomic number counts protons; mass number counts protons plus neutrons."],
    "s9-matter": ["Atoms join to form molecules. Chemists count particles in moles: one mole is about 6.022 times 10 to the 23.", "Water, H2O, has molecular mass 2 times 1 plus 16, which is 18 u.", "Mass is conserved in a chemical reaction."],
    "s9-sound": ["Sound is a longitudinal wave made by vibrations. It needs a medium and can't travel in a vacuum.", "Humans hear from 20 to 20,000 hertz. Bats use ultrasound above that range.", "Pitch depends on frequency; loudness depends on amplitude."],
    "s9-repro": ["Organisms reproduce to continue their kind. Asexual reproduction needs one parent; sexual reproduction joins two gametes.", "Amoeba splits by binary fission, yeast reproduces by budding, and flowering plants need pollination and fertilisation.", "Sexual reproduction creates more variation."],
    "s9-class": ["Living things are classified into groups by shared features. Each species gets a two-part scientific name.", "Humans are Homo sapiens. Mushrooms are fungi, not plants.", "The genus name starts with a capital letter; the species name does not."],
    "s9-earth": ["Earth is a system of four spheres: land, water, air and life. Energy from the Sun drives cycles of water, carbon and nitrogen.", "Plants take carbon dioxide from the air in photosynthesis; respiration and burning return it.", "Matter is recycled in ecosystems, but energy flows through and is lost as heat."],
    "s10-metal": ["Metals lose electrons to form positive ions. The reactivity series ranks metals from potassium down to gold.", "Iron displaces copper from copper sulphate because iron is more reactive.", "Gold and silver are found free in nature because they are least reactive."],
    "s10-carbon": ["Carbon forms covalent bonds by sharing electrons. It can link to itself in long chains, called catenation.", "Alkanes follow C n H 2n plus 2: methane is CH4 and ethane is C2H6.", "Soap forms micelles that trap oily dirt."],
    "s10-ctrl": ["Animals use the nervous system and hormones to control the body. Plants use hormones like auxin.", "Pulling your hand from a hot object is a reflex action controlled by the spinal cord.", "Insulin comes from the pancreas; thyroxine needs iodine."],
    "s10-repro": ["Reproduction makes copies of DNA, which creates variation. Organisms reproduce sexually or asexually.", "Rhizopus spreads by spores. In flowers, the ovary becomes the fruit and ovules become seeds.", "The stamen is male and the pistil is female."],
    "s10-hered": ["Traits pass from parents to children through genes. Mendel showed that dominant traits hide recessive ones.", "Tall and dwarf pea plants give all tall in F1, then 3 tall to 1 dwarf in F2.", "The father's X or Y chromosome decides the child's sex."],
    "s10-eye": ["The eye focuses light on the retina. Accommodation lets it adjust for near and far objects.", "Myopia is corrected with a concave lens, and hypermetropia with a convex lens.", "The sky is blue because air scatters blue light more than red light."],
    "s10-mag": ["A current creates a magnetic field. The right-hand thumb rule gives its direction.", "Fleming's left-hand rule gives the force on a wire in a motor. A changing magnetic field induces a current.", "A fuse melts to break the circuit if too much current flows."],
    "s10-env": ["An ecosystem has producers, consumers and decomposers. Only about 10 percent of energy passes to the next level.", "Harmful chemicals like pesticides build up along a food chain; this is biological magnification.", "Plastics are non-biodegradable; peels and paper are biodegradable."],
    "e6-u1": ["Fables are short tales, often with animals, that end with a moral. Folk tales are passed down by word of mouth.", "In 'The Raven and the Fox', the fox flatters the raven so it opens its beak and drops the cheese. The moral: don't trust flatterers.", "Use 'an' before vowel sounds: an honest fox, a hungry raven."],
    "e6-u2": ["This unit is about friendship. Nouns name people, places and things; pronouns replace them.", "'Rahul is my friend. He plays cricket.' Here 'He' replaces 'Rahul'.", "Proper nouns, like Jaipur, start with a capital letter."],
    "e6-u3": ["This unit celebrates nature: trees like neem and healing spices. Synonyms are words with similar meanings.", "'Nurture' means to care for, and 'tiny' means 'small'.", "A synonym must fit the same sentence without changing its meaning."],
    "e6-u4": ["This unit is about sports and wellness. Antonyms are opposites, and good sportsmanship means respecting others.", "The opposite of 'victory' is 'defeat'. International Day of Yoga is on 21 June.", "Pick the antonym that matches the word's part of speech."],
    "e6-u5": ["This unit explores India's culture and traditions, like kite festivals and the National War Memorial in New Delhi.", "Ila Sachani creates embroidery using her feet, showing great determination.", "Learn tricky spellings in parts: tra-di-tion, gov-ern-ment."],
    "e7-u1": ["This unit is about learning together. Tenses show when an action happens.", "'I have finished my homework' uses the present perfect for something just completed.", "Helen Keller, who wrote 'Three Days To See', could neither see nor hear."],
    "e7-u2": ["This unit brings wit and humour. Adjectives describe nouns; adverbs describe verbs.", "'She sings beautifully': 'beautifully' is an adverb telling how she sings.", "Comparatives use 'er' or 'more'; never use both, like 'more faster'."],
    "e7-u3": ["This unit is about dreams and discoveries, including Tagore's poem 'Paper Boats'.", "In 'Paper Boats', a child floats boats down a stream, dreaming they will reach faraway lands.", "Spell carefully: 'necessary' has one c and two s's."],
    "e7-u4": ["This unit is about travel and adventure. Prepositions show place, time and direction.", "'The train went through the tunnel.' 'Through' shows movement inside something.", "Use 'since' with a point in time and 'for' with a length of time."],
    "e7-u5": ["This unit honours brave hearts like Rani Abbakka, who fought the Portuguese.", "'Courageous' is a synonym of 'brave'.", "A homage is a show of respect."],
    "e8-u1": ["This unit is about wit and wisdom. Idioms are phrases whose meaning differs from the words themselves.", "'A piece of cake' means very easy. 'Burn the midnight oil' means work late.", "Don't read idioms literally."],
    "e8-u2": ["This unit is about values like courage and kindness. In the passive voice, the object becomes the subject.", "'The king praised the minister' becomes 'The minister was praised by the king'.", "Keep the tense the same: past active becomes past passive."],
    "e8-u3": ["This unit is about mystery and magic. Homophones sound alike but differ in meaning and spelling.", "'They're going there with their friends.' Each word sounds the same but means something different.", "'Hear' is to listen; 'here' is a place."],
    "e8-u4": ["This unit is about the environment, including Ruskin Bond's 'The Cherry Tree'. The verb must agree with its subject.", "'Each of the students has a book.' 'Each' is singular, so it takes 'has'.", "'Either… or' and 'neither… nor' agree with the nearer subject."],
    "e8-u5": ["This unit is about science and curiosity: Arthur C. Clarke's 'Feathered Friend' and physicist Bibha Chowdhuri.", "In 'Feathered Friend', the canary Claribel warns the crew when oxygen runs low.", "Learn new words from context."],
    "e9-kav": ["Kaveri is the new Class 9 English textbook. 'How I Taught My Grandmother to Read' is by Sudha Murty.", "Her grandmother learned to read at 62 so she could read the novel 'Kashi Yatre' herself.", "The lesson: it's never too late to learn."],
    "e10-ffp": ["First Flight poems include Robert Frost's 'Dust of Snow' and 'Fire and Ice'.", "In 'Dust of Snow', a crow shakes snow onto the poet, which lifts his mood.", "In 'Fire and Ice', fire stands for desire and ice for hatred."],
    "e10-fp": ["Footprints without Feet is the supplementary reader with stories like 'The Thief's Story' and 'The Necklace'.", "Griffin, a scientist, became invisible. Matilda borrowed a necklace from Madame Forestier.", "Bholi's real name was Sulekha."],
  });
})(window.GA_LESSONS);
